// optfault.nv — the six ways a minimisation is refused before it
// starts, and why none of them is "it did not converge".
//
// NOT CONVERGING IS NOT A FAILURE.  An iteration that runs out of
// budget has produced a point, a function value and a count, and a
// caller can use all three — to restart from there, to report progress,
// to decide the model is good enough.  Throwing that away and
// returning an error loses the work.  So the outcome of a run is an
// `OptReport` with a status on it, and the status has an arm for the
// budget and an arm for a stall.  `optstop.nv`'s module comment is
// where that is argued.
//
// WHAT IS LEFT FOR THIS ENUM is the set of calls that describe no
// minimisation at all: a starting point with no coordinates, a
// bracket that does not bracket, a stopping rule that could never
// stop, a simplex of the wrong size.  Every one of them is a mistake
// in the CALL rather than a fact about the function, and every one of
// them is worth finding before the first evaluation rather than after
// the thousandth.

// Why a minimisation could not be attempted.
//
// ```novo
// use optfault
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = OptEmptyStart
//     println(e.message())
// ```
pub enum OptFault
    // The starting point has no coordinates, so there is nothing to
    // vary.
    OptEmptyStart
    // The initial simplex does not have `n + 1` vertices for an
    // `n`-dimensional problem, or two of its vertices are the same
    // point.  Carries what was given and what was needed.
    OptBadSimplex(given: Int, needed: Int)
    // The bracket handed to a one-dimensional method does not bracket
    // a minimum: `lower` is not below `upper`, or the function at the
    // interior point is not below both ends.  Carries the interval.
    OptBadBracket(lower: Float, upper: Float)
    // The stopping rule could never stop: every tolerance is zero and
    // no budget is set, so the iteration would run for ever.  `field`
    // names what to set.
    OptStopUnreachable(field: Str)
    // A tolerance or a step is negative, or a budget is not positive.
    // `field` names it and `value` carries what was given.
    OptBadSetting(field: Str, value: Float)
    // The residual function answered a vector of a different length
    // than it did the first time, so the least-squares problem is not
    // one problem.  Carries both lengths.
    OptResidualLengthChanged(first: Int, later: Int)
    // A gradient the caller supplied has a different length than the
    // point it was taken at.  Carries both.
    OptGradientLengthWrong(point: Int, gradient: Int)

// `OptFault` is an `Error`, which is what lets `Result<T, OptFault>`
// exist at all (SPEC § 3.4).
impl Error for OptFault
    // The one-line message: what about the call was wrong, and the
    // numbers that make it wrong.
    //
    // Panics with `not implemented` until the bodies land.
    fn message(self) -> Str
        todo("optimize-nv.OptFault.message")
